WEBVTT

NOTE
Canonical written narration timed from the exact local narration render receipt.
Pronunciation-only substitutions are intentionally absent from caption text.

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.972
This chapter asks a specific question: A learned artifact creates attack

2
00:00:04.972 --> 00:00:08.990
surfaces that ordinary application security does not fully own.

3
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:14.225
Attackers can shape training data, perturb inference inputs, implant triggers,

4
00:00:14.225 --> 00:00:19.121
adapt to defenses, steal behavior through queries, infer sensitive properties,

5
00:00:19.121 --> 00:00:24.506
or exploit multimodal and agentic pathways while the surrounding software remains

6
00:00:24.506 --> 00:00:25.485
nominally secure.

7
00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:30.767
The tempting shortcut is insufficient: Generic access control, one robustness score,

8
00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:35.709
static red teaming, accuracy on clean data, or a single defense

9
00:00:35.709 --> 00:00:37.955
cannot characterize a model attack.

10
00:00:38.555 --> 00:00:43.729
The chapter's core claim is this: A learned model should receive

11
00:00:43.729 --> 00:00:48.904
security authority only through a versioned model-threat contract and attack/defense ledger

12
00:00:48.904 --> 00:00:54.078
that binds checkpoint identity, lifecycle stage, attacker knowledge and capability, surface,

13
00:00:54.078 --> 00:00:55.960
budget, objective, adaptation, transfer,

14
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:02.034
observed effect, detection, mitigation, utility cost, recovery, residual, and disclosure;

15
00:01:02.034 --> 00:01:06.622
clean accuracy, attack failure, benchmark robustness, red-team coverage,

16
00:01:06.622 --> 00:01:12.930
or formal certification alone establishes neither general robustness nor secure deployment.

17
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:17.691
Version the threat model by model family, exact checkpoint,

18
00:01:17.691 --> 00:01:23.094
data and adaptation lineage, modality, lifecycle stage, access, attacker goal, knowledge,

19
00:01:23.094 --> 00:01:25.550
budget, and prohibited real-world effects.

20
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:32.565
Maintain separate evasion, poisoning, backdoor, safeguard-bypass, extraction, inversion, transfer, adaptive, multimodal,

21
00:01:32.565 --> 00:01:37.420
and agentic attack lanes with complete attempt denominators.

22
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.700
A concrete implementation trace makes the proposal testable.

23
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.760
Implement a model-threat record and a safe evaluation harness over public

24
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.600
toy or consented models.

25
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.679
Include clean, random-noise, known-vulnerable, attack-aware, transfer, and adaptive controls;

26
00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:59.471
preserve query and tuning budgets; and report robustness with clean utility,

27
00:01:59.471 --> 00:02:05.264
false positives, recovery, latency, and residuals. No result transfers automatically to

28
00:02:05.264 --> 00:02:07.370
frontier models or production.

29
00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:14.385
Maintain separate evasion, poisoning, backdoor, safeguard-bypass, extraction, inversion, transfer, adaptive, multimodal,

30
00:02:14.385 --> 00:02:19.240
and agentic attack lanes with complete attempt denominators.

31
00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.150
Evaluate defenses against adaptive attacks and matched clean utility;

32
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:29.735
distinguish empirical monitoring, recovery, and bounded certificates by their actual scope.

33
00:02:30.335 --> 00:02:35.502
The design can still fail. evasion training-data poisoning clean-label poisoning backdoors

34
00:02:35.502 --> 00:02:40.668
and trojans The correct response is to stop, narrow, quarantine, compensate,

35
00:02:40.668 --> 00:02:45.365
or retain an owned residual rather than narrate uncertainty away.

36
00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:51.011
This chapter remains Design rationale at argument support. Its contracts, examples,

37
00:02:51.011 --> 00:02:55.140
tests, or local artifacts establish only their encoded scope.

38
00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:00.186
The chapter names proof targets rather than pretending they are already

39
00:03:00.186 --> 00:03:00.645
closed.

40
00:03:00.985 --> 00:03:05.969
A finite Adversarial Machine Learning and the Model Attack Surface record

41
00:03:05.969 --> 00:03:10.500
may hand off only when identity, authority, version, required checks,

42
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.484
and residual ownership are present; no theorem grants empirical effectiveness or

43
00:03:15.484 --> 00:03:16.390
release authority.

44
00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:21.653
A defense is evaluated against an attacker aware of that defense

45
00:03:21.653 --> 00:03:26.129
whenever the threat model permits. Clean utility, attacked utility, detection,

46
00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:29.710
false positives, recovery, and cost remain jointly visible.

47
00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:35.274
No chapter claim or evidence state is promoted by this visual.

48
00:03:35.274 --> 00:03:40.238
A named mechanism is not proof of correct implementation or useful

49
00:03:40.238 --> 00:03:45.202
deployment. A local check or formal model does not establish real-world

50
00:03:45.202 --> 00:03:49.715
enforcement. No safety, transfer, state-of-the-art, AGI, or ASI conclusion follows.

51
00:03:50.055 --> 00:03:54.530
These boundaries matter because an explanatory derivative is useful only if

52
00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.785
